// @test: run
// @tier: 0
// @level: L1
// @tags: names, collision, codegen, T0946
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-stdout-begin
// control=42
// resolve=42
// @expected-stdout-end
// @description: A locally declared function whose simple name collides with a stdlib one of the SAME arity keeps its own return type

// `resolve` collides with `core.encoding.json_pointer.resolve`, which
// takes two arguments and returns `Maybe<JsonValue>`. Before T0946 the
// local one printed `None`.
//
// The value was never wrong — `v == 42` held and `v + 0` printed 42.
// Only the recorded TYPE NAME came from the wrong function, so an `Int`
// formatted as a `Maybe` rendered `None`.
//
// ROOT: the registration writes two keys with OPPOSITE collision
// policies. `functions[name]` is last-wins, so it held the local
// function correctly; `functions[name#arity]` was created with
// `or_insert` — first-wins — for the bake's byte-determinism, and its
// comment claimed "extra mirrors are semantically inert". They are not:
// the arity-aware lookup reads them.
//
// BOTH lines are pinned, and the second is the one that discriminates.
// A first draft of this file expected only `control=42` — a line that
// is green before AND after the repair, so the spec would have passed
// on the broken compiler. `resolve=42` is the claim.
//
// `uniquely_named_thing` is the control. It is the same function with a
// name nothing collides with, so a repair that breaks name resolution
// outright fails here first.

type Holder is { id: Int };
type Key is { k: Int };

fn resolve(h: &List<Holder>, k: &Key) -> Int {
    k.k + h.len()
}

fn uniquely_named_thing(h: &List<Holder>, k: &Key) -> Int {
    k.k + h.len()
}

fn main() {
    let hs = [Holder { id: 1 }, Holder { id: 2 }];
    let k = Key { k: 40 };
    print(f"control={uniquely_named_thing(&hs, &k)}");
    print(f"resolve={resolve(&hs, &k)}");
}
